\ A word with a row among its inputs runs compiled whatever the row holds, including nothing. The
\ row is not a stack slot, so a call with only `n` on the stack is as valid as one with a value
\ under it. The trace shows a dispatch hit on all three calls.

row-plain: ( ..a n -- ..a n ) [ 1 + ] ;
row-plain: compile!

0 3 row-plain . .
3 row-plain .
0 3 row-plain . .
